title: The redraw delta
pid: 572a7445-7205-47d3-8aee-2d8b5bf90903
body: |2-
  A response that redraws a view may name the headlines whose text
  changed, so that the client rewrites only those lines: the entry
  =(changed-headlines (V1 V2 ...))=, whose Vs are viewnode IDs (bare
  symbols, none read as a number) in the new text's order. Its absence
  means "whole": the client redraws the whole buffer. Present and
  empty, =(changed-headlines ())=, it means no headline changed. Only
  the =save-result= carries it (see "Save buffer"); the server
  computes it in 'server/view_text_delta.rs'. It never changes the
  text sent: =content= is the whole new text, fold and focus atoms
  included.

  The server compares the new text with a reference text, for the
  =save-result= the saved buffer's text. It splits each into a
  preamble, the text before the first headline line, and headline
  records: a headline line (one matching '^\*+ '; the server escapes a
  body line that would match with a leading comma,
  'encode_body_for_view' in 'server/body_view_escape.rs') with the
  body lines after it, up to the next headline line or the
  end. The viewnode ID of a headline line is the X of a line beginning
  =^\*+ (skg (vnid X)=. The delta is "whole" when the preambles differ,
  when the record counts differ, when some headline line of either
  text has no viewnode ID, when the sequences of viewnode IDs differ,
  or when the body lines of some record differ (a headline line's
  own newline counts with its body). Otherwise it lists each viewnode
  ID whose stripped headline lines differ, perhaps none.

  The stripped form of a headline line is the line with each of the
  bare atoms =focused=, =folded= and =bodyFolded= that stands at depth
  1 of its =(skg ...)= form removed, together with the one space before
  it. The form is the one right after the stars and a space, beginning
  =(skg= and then a space or =)=. Depth counts parentheses outside
  string literals (a literal runs from a double quote to the next
  unescaped one, a backslash escaping the character after it); a bare
  atom is a maximal run of characters that are not whitespace,
  parentheses or double quotes. A line without such a form is its own
  stripped form, as is any line that is not a headline line. The
  stripped form of a text strips each of its headline lines and leaves
  its body lines and preamble alone. The server and both clients
  implement this rule, and test it against the cases in
  'tests/shared/fold-and-focus-atom-strip-cases.txt'.

  A client given a delta patches the buffer instead of redrawing it.
  It replaces each listed headline line, found by its viewnode ID, by
  that viewnode ID's line in the stripped form of the new text,
  inserted as text; re-heralds the replaced lines, the folders among
  their two nearest ancestors and the headlines one or two levels
  below them (whose heralds read them); and then compares the whole
  buffer with the stripped form of the new text. On success it keeps
  its folds and point, marks the buffer unmodified, takes the
  stripped form as the view's baseline (see "The baseline of a view")
  and refreshes the link annotations. It redraws the whole buffer
  from the new text instead when a listed viewnode ID is missing from
  the buffer or from the new text, when the comparison finds any
  difference (a line the client reprinted otherwise than the server,
  say), or, in Emacs, when 'org-fold-core-style' is not 'overlays'
  (under 'text-properties' a replaced headline above a folded body
  would hide itself). Both clients do this for the =save-result=:
  Emacs in 'skg--patch-buffer-with-delta'
  ('elisp/skg-redraw-delta.el'), Neovim in 'patch_buffer_with_delta'
  ('nvim/lua/skg/redraw_delta.lua'). Each rewrites only the span of a
  line between the prefix and the suffix the old and new line share,
  so the cursor keeps its character on the line; in Neovim this also
  keeps the vim folds, since replacing a whole line
  ('nvim_buf_set_lines') opens closed expr folds where replacing a
  span ('nvim_buf_set_text') does not. A Neovim view opened from text
  acts on that text's fold and focus atoms as a whole redraw does,
  removing them, so its first save can be patched; the Emacs opener
  keeps them as text ('TODO/problems.org').
